{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_104","type":"reasoning","text":"Interesting distribution:\n- 17,426 variants with AF 1.0 → homozygous alt (simulated homozygous variants).\n- 755 with AF 0.35–0.65 → heterozygous (AF ~0.5).\n- Low AF tail: 181 variants with AF < 0.35 (mosaic candidates!), 410 with AF > 0.65 (mosaic on the reference side? or alt-biased het).\n\nSo the simulated genome is: ~17.4k homozygous variants + ~755 het variants + low-level mosaic candidates. The mosaic nonsense variant should be among the AF<0.35 (or 0.65–0.95) ones.\n\nAlright, annotation is needed. Plan:\n1. Build annotation engine (GTF + FASTA).\n2. Annotate all 18,787 called variants (or at least all SNVs), find stop_gained ones.\n3. Cross-check with allsites.tsv to get accurate counts at those positions.\n4. Identify mosaic AF + stop_gained + protein_coding + LoF-intolerant gene.\n\nNow let's write the full annotation engine. Key components:\n\nA. Parse GTF:\n- For each transcript with transcript_type == \"protein_coding\" (and gene_type == \"protein_coding\"):\n  - Collect CDS entries (chrom, start, end, strand, frame attribute?), gene_name, gene_id, transcript_id, transcript_name.\n  - GTF has a \"frame\" column (phase) for CDS entries — can be used directly for reading frame determination.\n- Also record gene intervals for overlap queries.\n\nB. Consequence computation for SNV at position p (1-based), ref r, alt a:\n- For each protein-coding transcript:\n  - If p is within an exon's CDS segment: compute offset from CDS start: sort CDS segments by position (in strand order); offset = sum of lengths of preceding segments + (p - seg_start) (forward strand) or (seg_end - p) (reverse strand). Codon index = offset // 3; position within codon = offset % 3.\n  - Reference codon: extract 3 bases at CDS coordinates: codon_start_offset = codon_index * 3; collect bases at offsets codon_start_offset .. +2 (in CDS orientation). Build codon; reverse complement if minus strand.\n  - Translate ref and alt codons with standard codon table.\n  - If ref AA is an amino acid and alt AA is '*': stop_gained (nonsense). Check that it's premature: codon_index < total_codons - 1 (i.e., not the natural stop codon position). Note: natural stop codon in GENCODE CDS: is the stop codon included in the CDS feature? In GENCODE, yes — the stop codon is typically included in the CDS region (the last 3 bases). So if the variant hits the natural stop codon and makes it a different stop or amino acid: stop_lost or synonymous stop. For nonsense, require codon_index < n_codons - 1 (strictly premature).\n  - Other consequences: missense, synonymous, stop_lost, etc. Also splice site (within ±2 of exon boundary) — but for the task focus on stop_gained.\n\nC. Mosaic candidate selection:\n- From called variants: SNVs with AF in (0.02, 0.98), but specifically excluding clear germline het (0.4–0.6)? The task specifies mosaic; in the simulated data the mosaic AF is probably distinctly different from 0.5 (e.g., ~0.1–0.3).\n- Stop_gained consequence in a protein_coding gene.\n- High-confidence filters: DP ≥ 20, alt reads ≥ 5, AF in some range, alt supported on both strands (ADF>0 and ADR>0), MQ OK (already filtered with -q 30), no nearby variants within the same read? (skip), base quality OK (filtered with -Q 20).\n\nD. LoF intolerance: for the gene in question, fetch gnomAD constraint (pLI, LOEUF) via gnomAD GraphQL API over the network. Also, might compare against all genes in the region... The report should record the gene's pLI/LOEUF. Let's fetch from gnomAD API (https://gnomad.broadinstitute.org/api) with GraphQL query { gene(symbol:\"X\"){ gnomadConstraint { expLoF obsLoF lofZ pLi lofOe lofOeCiUpper ... } } }. This requires network from WSL or Windows. Let's try.\n\nAlternative offline: use gene names — if the gene is a famous haploinsufficient one (e.g., TSC1, NOTCH1, ENG, TGFBR1/2...). Verify with gnomAD if network allows.\n\nNow let's write the annotation script. First a test: how many protein-coding transcripts are in the chr9 GTF?\n\nAlso for allsites.tsv (16.3M rows) — I want to restrict to called variant positions + a scan for positions with AF in mosaic range. Let's do a separate scan: from allsites.tsv, extract sites where alt count ≥3, DP ≥ 15, and 0.03 ≤ AF ≤ 0.97. Then annotate those. This catches mosaic variants missed by the caller.\n\nActually, the AD in allsites.tsv comes from INFO/AD, which is only ref,alt (2 values) — from bcftools mpileup -a INFO/AD... hmm, if the site is invariant, AD might be missing? I output with awk; some rows may have empty ad. In the next step, let me quickly check the format of a few rows.\n\nAnother consideration: the mosaic variant might have multiple ALT alleles; keep it simple: consider each ALT separately.\n\nNow let's write the main annotation + discovery Python script. This will become the core of the final output/analysis.py (refactored into a clean pipeline script).\n\nFirst, let's check the format of allsites.tsv."}}
