{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_120","type":"reasoning","text":"TSC1's CDS reconstructs perfectly: starts with ATG, length is a multiple of 3, no internal stops — but the last codon isn't a stop codon (\"last codon: GATGTGGAAGGT\" — hmm, so the last 12 bases; the final codon is GGT?). Wait: the last codon is cds[-3:] = \"GGT\"? So the CDS doesn't include the stop codon. prot tail \"PDVEG\" — ends with G, not *. So does GENCODE v47's TSC1 CDS annotation not include the stop codon? Interesting — actually, in GENCODE the stop codon is normally included in the CDS, but... hmm, for this transcript (ENST00000643625.2) the stop codon might be missing (transcripts tagged \"cds_end_NF\"). So my strict check `prot[-1] == '*'` fails on a lot of transcripts — maybe even all of them if GENCODE v47 handles stop codons differently... no wait, actually that's not right — GENCODE usually includes the stop in the CDS. But for this TSC1 transcript it clearly doesn't. Hmm, maybe because the 3' end is incomplete?\n\nLet me reconsider: relax the ok check: require that it starts with M and has no internal stops; don't require a trailing stop. Mark the stop position as known if prot[-1]=='*', otherwise n_codons = len(prot) (no annotated stop → any stop-gained within it is premature by definition).\n\nThis also affects the stop_gained determination: `codon_idx < t.n_codons - 1` — if the stop isn't annotated, all stop_gained are premature. If it is annotated, exclude the last codon.\n\nAlso fix the minus-strand slice bug (seq[e-1:s] → seq[s-1:e]) and GeneIndex's sort key.\n\nThen rerun."}}
