{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_126","type":"reasoning","text":"Excellent! The stop_gained candidates are as follows:\n\n1. GRIN3A pos=101670953 C>T, AF=0.550 (11/20) — looks like a heterozygous germline variant (AF ~0.5).\n2. ALAD pos=113391620 A>G, AF=0.619 (13/21) — close to heterozygous, slightly biased.\n3. COL27A1 pos=114196007 C>T, AF=0.182 (4/22) — low AF but only 4 ALT reads — weak.\n4. C5 pos=121017727 G>A, AF=0.475 (19/40) — heterozygous germline-like.\n5. **STXBP1 pos=127661125 G>T, AF=0.128 (6/47)** — clearly mosaic-like AF! Present in many transcripts (canonical E103*).\n\nSTXBP1 (syntaxin-binding protein 1) is a well-known haploinsufficiency / LoF-intolerant gene: related to early infantile epileptic encephalopathy (EIEE4), developmental delay, Ohtahara syndrome. gnomAD pLI = 1.0, LOEUF is very low (~0.06–0.1). STXBP1 is located at chr9q34.11.\n\nAF 0.128, 6 ALT reads out of 47 — this is a mosaic nonsense variant.\n\nHowever, 6 ALT reads is a bit low for \"high confidence\". Let me verify:\n- Duplicate reads: I excluded duplicates. Including duplicates might increase the ALT count. Let me check.\n- Strand balance: need ADF/ADR.\n- Also check with the raw BAM: visualize with samtools mpileup.\n- Check the surrounding sequence context (not a homopolymer).\n- Check base quality, mapping position.\n\nAlso, I'll check whether there are other stop_gained candidates at lower AF (my filter was ac>=3). To be safe, let me also scan with ac>=2. Furthermore, I'll verify whether STXBP1's AF is consistent between duplicate-included and duplicate-excluded data.\n\nAdditionally, GRIN3A, ALAD, and C5 with AF~0.5 are probably simulated germline heterozygous variants — these are not mosaic. COL27A1 is AF 0.18 with 4/22 — hmm, this is also a mosaic-like AF. COL27A1 — is it LoF-intolerant? COL27A1's pLI is probably low (~0); collagens are generally recessive. STXBP1 is extremely LoF-intolerant (pLI=1). The task states \"high-confidence mosaic nonsense SNV within a highly loss-of-function (LoF)-intolerant protein-coding gene\" (singular). Therefore STXBP1 is the answer — however, let me rigorously verify high-confidence with strand balance, duplicate-included, base quality, and also confirm the absence of nearby variants within the same reads, etc.\n\nCOL27A1's 4/22 might be noise (low AF, low count, possibly strand-biased). Let me get the details of both.\n\nAlso, I'll check whether the STXBP1 variant is in calls.vcf (it might have been filtered out by the caller due to low AF). And verify the sequence context.\n\nNext steps:\n1. Run a targeted deep pileup at chr9:127661125 (and 114196007) using both the duplicate-excluded and duplicate-included BAMs, obtaining ADF/ADR, base quality, and mapping quality.\n2. Check the reference context for homopolymers/repeats.\n3. Verify there are no nearby variants (within 10bp)."}}
